{"id": 4334, "date": "2017-08-20T01:40:53", "date_gmt": "2017-08-20T05:40:53", "guid": {"rendered": "http://ravencypresswood.com/?p=4334"}, "modified": "2017-08-20T08:22:52", "modified_gmt": "2017-08-20T12:22:52", "slug": "the-field-of-accumulation-the-yidams", "status": "publish", "type": "post", "link": "https://ravencypresswood.com/2017/08/20/the-field-of-accumulation-the-yidams/", "title": {"rendered": "The Field of Accumulation: The Yidams"}, "content": {"rendered": "<div id=\"attachment_2536\" style=\"width: 605px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-2536\" data-attachment-id=\"2536\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2014/12/06/the-field-of-accumulation-abbots-of-menri-monastery/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?fit=1580%2C1976&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1580,1976\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"Tsok zhing according to Menri\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?fit=819%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\" wp-image-2536\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?resize=595%2C744&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"Tsok zhing according to Menri\" width=\"595\" height=\"744\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-2536\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Tsok zhing according to MenriThe Yungdrung Bon Merit Field of the Menri Tradition</p></div>\n<p>In the Yungdrung Bön tradition, the place where the deities and objects of veneration are gathered is called the <span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong><em>&#8220;Tsok Zhing&#8221;, </em></strong></span>the <span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>&#8220;Field of Accumulation&#8221;. </strong> </span>It is also sometimes translated as the <strong><span style=\"color:#000080;\">&#8220;Field of Merit&#8221;</span></strong> or the <span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>&#8220;Merit Field&#8221;</strong></span> because what is being accumulated by paying homage and making offerings to this place is merit, or virtue.  This is the Merit Field according to the Menri Tradition from a drawing done by the great master and scholar HE Yongdzin Tenzin Namdak Rinpoche.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>Yidams</strong></span> are deities who have their own individual cycles of practice with the aim of acquiring the qualities and blessings of the yidam being meditated upon.  In general, these cycles of practice are centered around an individual retreat in which the practitioner focuses upon self-transformation into the deity, recitation of the deity&#8217;s mantra, along with any other ritual or meditation specific to the deity which support the attainment of the deities qualities and blessings.  There are yidams specific to a family lineage, to monastic lineages as well as personal yidams.  Some of the vows associated with yidam practice are general to all, and some vows are specific to each yidam.  Some yidams are represented more than once to signify their different tantric cycles.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-yidams.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-attachment-id=\"4348\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2017/08/20/the-field-of-accumulation-the-yidams/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-yidams/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-yidams.jpg?fit=1166%2C1556&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1166,1556\" data-comments-opened=\"1\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"Outline guide for tsok zhing YIDAMS\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-yidams.jpg?fit=767%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"aligncenter wp-image-4348\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-yidams.jpg?resize=702%2C937&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" width=\"702\" height=\"937\" /></a></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">#2-4, 14 &amp; 18: The Five Supreme Embodiments of the Father Tantra (See previous post:<a href=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2016/06/05/the-five-supreme-embodiments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://ravencypresswood.com/2016/06/05/the-five-supreme-embodiments/</a>)</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">1. Zhiwa Künnang Khyappa</span>: This yidam represents all peaceful yidams</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">2. Trowo Tsochok Khagying</span>: This yidam is an embodiment of the Enlightened Mind of Buddha Tönpa Shenrap Miwoche.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">3. Walsé Ngampa</span>: This yidam is an embodiment of the Enlightened Body of Buddha Tönpa Shenrap Miwoche.</p>\n<div id=\"attachment_4341\" style=\"width: 673px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/walse-ngampa-at-bon-gya-edit.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-4341\" data-attachment-id=\"4341\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2017/08/20/the-field-of-accumulation-the-yidams/walse-ngampa-at-bon-gya-edit-2/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/walse-ngampa-at-bon-gya-edit.jpg?fit=903%2C612&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"903,612\" data-comments-opened=\"1\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"Walse Ngampa at Bon Gya edit\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/walse-ngampa-at-bon-gya-edit.jpg?fit=903%2C612&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"wp-image-4341 \" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/walse-ngampa-at-bon-gya-edit.jpg?resize=663%2C449&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" width=\"663\" height=\"449\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-4341\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">The Yidam Walse Ngampa, Embodiment of the Enl ightened Body</p></div>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">4. Lhago Tokpa</span>: This yidam is an embodiment of the Enlightened Speech of Buddha Tönpa Shenrap Miwoche.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">5. Sangwa Ying Rol</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">6. Tséwang Rikdzin:</span> As a long life deity, this yidam is white in color.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">7. Magyü Sangchok Tartuk:</span> This yidam is the principal deity of the Mother Tantra and is also known as Tukjé Galpo.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">8. Drenpa Namkha</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">9. Bumpa</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">10. Rampa</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">11. Rolpa</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">12. Dütsi Yungdrung Khyilwa</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">13. Takla Pudri Marpo</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">14. Gekho Sangwa Drakchen:</span>  This yidam is the embodiment of the Enlightened Qualities of Buddha Tönpa Shenrap Miwoche.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">15. Trowo Druksé Chempa:</span> This yidam is the Embodiment of the Enlightened Activities of Buddha Tönpa Shenrap Miwoche.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">16. Meri Walchen Gekho</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">17. Chidul Yidam Gyatso Trogyal Raksha Khagying</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">18. Sangpur</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">19. Walsé Khyungnak Trowo Karpo</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">20.Wal Khyung Marpo</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\"><strong>Yidams of the Other Tantras</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">#22-#25 The Four Principal Enlightened Ones </span>(See previous post <a href=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2016/08/20/the-four-principal-enlightened-ones/\">https://ravencypresswood.com/2016/08/20/the-four-principal-enlightened-ones/</a>)</p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">21. Gyepa Kunnang Khyapa</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">22. Tönpa Shenrap Miwo</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">23. Sipa Sangpo Bumtri</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">24. Lhachen Shenlha Ökar</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">25. Yumchen Satrik Érsang</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">26. Shenrap Nampar Gyalwa</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">27. Namdak</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">28. Jamma</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">29. Mélha</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">30. Kéngtsé Lenmé</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">31. Sherab Mawé Séngé</span></p>\n<div id=\"attachment_4343\" style=\"width: 522px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/mawai-senge-white-with-border.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-4343\" data-attachment-id=\"4343\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2017/08/20/the-field-of-accumulation-the-yidams/mawai-senge-white-with-border/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/mawai-senge-white-with-border.jpg?fit=1080%2C1440&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1080,1440\" data-comments-opened=\"1\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"Mawai Senge White with border\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/mawai-senge-white-with-border.jpg?fit=768%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"wp-image-4343\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/mawai-senge-white-with-border.jpg?resize=512%2C683&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" width=\"512\" height=\"683\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-4343\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">The Yidam Mawe Senge</p></div>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">32. Dülchok Tönpa Tritsuk Gyalwa</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">33. Jamma</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">34. Namdak</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">35. Mönlam Taye</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">36. Menlha</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">37. Künying</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">38. Gényen Tékpa Lha</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">39. Jamden</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">40. Dükhor</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">41. Künrik</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">42. Gyalwa Gyatso</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"color:#000080;\">43. Namjom</span></p>\n<div id=\"attachment_4350\" style=\"width: 555px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/nampar-jompa-retinue-cropped.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-4350\" data-attachment-id=\"4350\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2017/08/20/the-field-of-accumulation-the-yidams/nampar-jompa-retinue-cropped/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/nampar-jompa-retinue-cropped.jpg?fit=2863%2C3672&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"2863,3672\" data-comments-opened=\"1\" data-image-title=\"Nampar Jompa &amp;amp; retinue cropped\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/nampar-jompa-retinue-cropped.jpg?fit=798%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"wp-image-4350\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/nampar-jompa-retinue-cropped.jpg?resize=545%2C699&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" width=\"545\" height=\"699\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-4350\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">The Yidam Namjom, aka Nampar Jompa, and his emanations.</p></div>\n", "protected": false}, "excerpt": {"rendered": "<p>In the Yungdrung Bön tradition, the place where the deities and objects of veneration are gathered is called the &#8220;Tsok Zhing&#8221;, the &#8220;Field of Accumulation&#8221;.  It is also sometimes translated as the &#8220;Field of Merit&#8221; or the &#8220;Merit Field&#8221; because what is being accumulated by paying homage and making offerings to this place is merit, or virtue.  [&hellip;]</p>\n", "protected": false}, "author": 44992789, "featured_media": 0, "comment_status": "open", "ping_status": "open", "sticky": false, "template": "", "format": "image", "meta": {"_coblocks_attr": "", "_coblocks_dimensions": "", "_coblocks_responsive_height": "", "_coblocks_accordion_ie_support": "", "advanced_seo_description": "", "jetpack_seo_html_title": "", "jetpack_seo_noindex": false, "jetpack_seo_schema_type": "", "_wpcom_ai_launchpad_first_post": false, "_jetpack_feature_clip_id": 0, "_jetpack_memberships_contains_paid_content": false, "footnotes": ""}, "categories": [69608, 30524130, 5909569, 1, 295386984], "tags": [], "class_list": ["post-4334", "post", "type-post", "status-publish", "format-image", "hentry", "category-iconography", "category-prayer-and-ritual", "category-tibetan-lamas", "category-uncategorized", "category-zhang-zhung-nyen-gyu", "post_format-post-format-image"], "jetpack_likes_enabled": true, "jetpack_sharing_enabled": true, "jetpack_shortlink": "https://wp.me/p35YG8-17U", "jet